# Accessibility (React)

Ship UI that works with keyboard, screen readers, and assistive tech by default. Prefer correct HTML semantics over ARIA patches. Be ready to explain tradeoffs out loud.

## Core principle

**Semantic HTML first → keyboard and focus next → ARIA only when HTML is not enough.**

Do not add `role` / `aria-*` to fix bad markup when a native element already provides the behavior.

## Semantics

- Use the correct element: `button`, `a`, `input`, `label`, `nav`, `main`, `header`, `ul`/`li`, headings in order.
- Do not make `div` / `span` clickable when a `button` or `a` is correct.
- Links navigate; buttons trigger actions. Do not mix them.
- One `h1` per view; do not skip heading levels for styling.
- Decorative images: empty `alt=""`. Meaningful images: descriptive `alt`.
- Icon-only controls need a name on the control (`aria-label` or visually hidden text). Mark the decorative icon `aria-hidden="true"` so assistive tech does not announce it twice or read SVG junk.

```tsx
// Bad
<div onClick={save}>Save</div>

// Good
<button type="button" onClick={save}>Save</button>

// Icon-only
<button type="button" aria-label="Close dialog" onClick={onClose}>
  <CloseIcon aria-hidden="true" />
</button>
```

## Keyboard and focus

- Every interactive control must be reachable and operable with keyboard (`Tab`, `Enter`/`Space`, arrows where expected).
- Do not remove focus outlines without a visible replacement (`:focus-visible`).
- Manage focus when UI appears or disappears (dialogs, menus, drawers, route changes).
- On dialog open: move focus into the dialog. On close: restore focus to the trigger.
- Trap focus inside modal dialogs; `Escape` should close when that is the platform pattern.
- After destructive or async actions, do not leave focus on a removed node.

## ARIA — use sparingly

- Prefer native elements over `role="button"`, `role="link"`, etc.
- Common valid uses: `aria-label` / `aria-labelledby`, `aria-describedby`, `aria-expanded`, `aria-controls`, `aria-invalid`, `aria-live` / `role="status"` / `role="alert"`.
- Keep `aria-*` in sync with real UI state (`aria-expanded` must match open/closed).
- Never rely on ARIA alone for behavior — keyboard handlers and focus still required.

## Forms

- Every input needs a visible `<label>` (or `aria-label` when a visible label is intentionally omitted).
- Associate errors with `aria-describedby` + `aria-invalid`.
- Do not use placeholder as the only label.
- Disable submit during pending only when double-submit is harmful; still announce status.

```tsx
<label htmlFor="email">Email</label>
<input
  id="email"
  aria-invalid={hasError}
  aria-describedby={hasError ? 'email-error' : undefined}
/>
{hasError && (
  <p id="email-error" role="alert">
    Enter a valid email
  </p>
)}
```

## Async and live updates

- Announce loading / saving / completion with `role="status"` or `aria-live="polite"`.
- Use `role="alert"` (or assertive live region) for important errors.
- Keep previous content visible while refreshing when possible; do not blank the whole page without announcement.
- Align with component-async rules: pending, success, empty, and error must each be perceivable.

## Motion, color, and contrast

- Do not convey meaning with color alone; add text or icons.
- Aim for WCAG AA contrast for text and essential UI.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` for non-essential animation.

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .logo {
    animation: none;
  }
}
```

## Testing accessibility

- Query by role and accessible name (`getByRole`, `getByLabelText`) — same as unit-test rules.
- Assert keyboard paths for custom widgets (open, close, focus return).
- Assert disabled / expanded / invalid states via accessible attributes, not class names.
- Cover icon-only controls by accessible name.
- Manual pass: keyboard-only tab through the flow; check focus order and announcements.

## Anti-patterns

- Clickable `div`/`span` without button/link semantics and keyboard support
- Missing accessible name on icon-only controls
- Broken or removed focus rings with no `:focus-visible` replacement
- Dialogs that do not move or restore focus
- Stale ARIA (`aria-expanded={true}` while closed)
- Placeholder-only “labels”
- Color-only status (red/green with no text)
- ARIA used to paper over incorrect HTML
